 rem Tremelles_TW

titre$  Morphological and Molecular Studies in the Genus Tremella
com$ tirée de  "http://faculty.stut.edu.tw/~c5200999/thesis.htm"  adapt 2008

1,cs1$ basidiocarps heterogeneously mixed with basidiomycetaceous, host hyphae up to the subhymenium, basidia mostly pyriform to capitate, subhymenial structure loose, haustoria abundant; $Aurantia group:$s"Aurantia group:"
 including T. aurantia, T. australiensis, T. encephala, T. microspora, and T. giraffa. 

Morphologically the Aurantia group is characterized by five features: 
1 )basidiocarps large or lacking (e. g. Tremellas in Dacrymyces); 
2) hymenial and subhymenial structures moderately loose; 
3) haustoria abundant, haustorial hyphae slender, rarely branched; 
4) host hyphae inter-mixing in the inner part, and subhymenium, or even hymenium of parasites; 
5) conidia, if existing, normally monokaryotic. 


1,cs2$ basidiocarps foliose and brown, basidia mostly oval, subhymenium with numerous anastomoses, terminally swollen cells in the inner parts of basidiocarps, haustoria rare; $Foliacea group$s"Foliacea group"
including T. foliacea sensu lato, T. fuscosuccinea, T. griseobrunnea, T. neofoliacea, T. mycophaga, T. simplex, and T. vasifera. 
1,cs3$ basidiocarps heterogeneously associated with Hypoxylon ascocarps, basidia mostly oval, subhymenium with numerous anastomoses, terminally swollen cells in the inner parts of basidiocarps, haustoria rare, haustorial hyphae heavily branched between the interface of parasites and hosts; $Fuciformis group$s"Fuciformis group"
 including T. flava, T. fuciformis, T. resupinata, and T. samoensis. 
1,cs4$ basidiocarps heterogeneously associated with pyreno- mycetaceous perithecia, basidia mostly pyriform to clavate, subhymenia loose, terminally cells in the inner parts of basidiocarps, haustoria rare,$Indecorata group:$s"Indecorata group:"
 including T. exigua, T. globospora, T. moriformis and T. nivalis. 
1,cs5$ basidiocarps gyrose to cerebriform, yellowish, basidia oval, hyphidia existing, subhymenial structure loose, terminal cells in the inner parts of basidiocarps lacking, haustoria abundant but haustorial hyphae heavily branched; $Mesenterica group$s"Mesenterica group"

including T. brasiliensis, T. cerebriformis, T. coalescens, T. mesenterica, T. taiwanensis, and T. tropica. 

